<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Del Carretto</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Del Carretto were a Ligurian and Piedmontese noble family descended from the Aleramici marquises of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a>. From the 12th century they ruled the Marquisate of Finale and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> on the western Riviera, with their seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finalborgo">Finalborgo</a>, while the port of Noli freed itself from them in 1192. Finale passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1602.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/del-carretto.jpg" alt="Del Carretto" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_House_of_Del_Carretto.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family descended from the House of Aleramici. Its founder was Henry del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasto">Vasto</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-del-vasto">Boniface del Vasto</a>, lord of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. Henry, Marquis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a> and one of the main collaborators of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, is often called Enrico I Del Carretto, although he never used that name. After his death, about 1185, his two sons, Otto and Henry, divided his lands, and their descendants split them further into many independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-riviera">Ligurian Riviera</a> and Lower Piedmont, at times joined in a league of Ghibelline leaning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1496 the Marquises of Finale mostly held the lead among the branches, with the title of imperial vicar for the marquisates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a> and Clavesana. The name appears in four forms: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s descendants often signed simply Carretto or Carretus, and the lower-case form del Carretto, common in older histories, was read as meaning a family from the village of Carretto near Cairo Montenotte, without any document to support it.</p><p>That reading served the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, which for centuries refused the family the title of marquises of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a>, though the family's own documents use it often. In 1642 the Genoese politician Raffaele Della Torre claimed in his Cyrologia that the name came from an imaginary marquisate of Carretto, and so that the family did not descend from Henry del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasto">Vasto</a>. The aim was to deny the Spanish kings any right to land at the roadstead of Vado or over the Savona area, rights resting on Philip II's agreements with the last marquis of Finale and renewed in Philip IV's investiture of 1639.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Members of the family held high office in church and state. Ottone del Carretto, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a> and podestà of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, was a patron of troubadours. Ilaria del Carretto was the second wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-guinigi">Paolo Guinigi</a>, Blanche del Carretto became Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a> by marriage, Carlo Domenico del Carretto was a papal legate and cardinal, and Fabrizio del Carretto was the 43rd Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Del Carretto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His descendants, the <b>Del Carretto</b>, made Finalborgo their seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/marquisate-of-finale">Marquisate of Finale</a></li><li><blockquote>Noli, a small port on the western Riviera, freed itself from the <b>Del Carretto</b> marquises and became a self-governing commune in 1192.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-noli">Republic of Noli</a></li></ul></section>
